# .trivyignore — waivers for Trivy findings you have reviewed and accepted.
#
# Add one CVE ID per line. Comments above the ID are part of the file's git
# history and become your audit trail for why a finding is not being fixed.
#
# Example:
#
#   # CVE-2025-12345 — libcurl in upstream base. Not reachable from our
#   # deployment (container runs behind mTLS ingress, no outbound HTTP).
#   # Revisit 2026-08 when upstream bumps the base image.
#   CVE-2025-12345
#
# Leave this file empty (except for comments) until you actually need a waiver.
# A waiver without a reason is just a mute button, not evidence.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reviewed 2026-11-17 for henrygd/beszel v0.21.2 (supersedes the v0.19.0 set,
# CVE issue #9). Default revisit: 2027-01-17.
#
# Deployment assumptions any future waiver here relies on:
#   - Image is the hub only: `scratch` + one static Go binary + CA certs.
#     No shell, no package manager, no JS runtime, no node_modules.
#   - Frontend JS exists only in the throwaway bun builder stage and ships as
#     minified assets embedded via go:embed.
#
# Current state: NO waivers. All previous entries were removed because they
# no longer match any finding at v0.21.2 (Trivy v0.70.0, as pinned by
# trivy-action v0.36.0, and v0.76.2 both report 0 CRITICAL/HIGH fixable):
#   - CVE-2026-4800 (lodash): bun.lock now resolves lodash 4.18.1 (fixed).
#   - CVE-2026-59869, GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj (js-yaml), CVE-2026-73088,
#     CVE-2026-73089 (browserslist): these were reported from
#     internal/site/package-lock.json, which now carries js-yaml 4.3.2 and
#     browserslist 4.29.3 (fixed). bun.lock still lists the old versions as
#     dev-only build tooling, which Trivy does not report without
#     --include-dev-deps (pipeline default does not set it).
# The v0.19.0 image findings (Go stdlib, golang.org/x/net) are fixed by the
# Go 1.27.4 bump in dockerfiles/Dockerfile.override and upstream's x/net
# v0.62.0, not waived.
